        <div class="faq-item">
          <button type="button" class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-q-text">Why 75 days? Why not 30?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <div class="faq-a-inner">
              <p>Your skin renews itself every 28 days. Three full cycles is what structural change actually requires. One cycle clears the surface. Two cycles rebuild the barrier. Three cycles is when the underlying biology shifts.</p><p>Products that promise results in 7 days are working on surface hydration or inflammation reduction. That's real, but it's not structural.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
    
  
    
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button type="button" class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-q-text">What should I expect at day 14, 28 and 75?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <div class="faq-a-inner">
              <p><strong>Day 1-14:</strong>&nbsp;Calibration. Your skin is adjusting, and you start to feel a surface-level softness. Most brands would celebrate this as the finish line. We see it as the starting line. The least any serum should do is make skin soft.</p><p><strong>Day 14-28:</strong>&nbsp;First signals. Texture begins to change. Hydration holds longer and the skin starts to look plumper, the foundation of glass skin. The people around you may notice something before you do.</p><p><strong>Day 28-60:</strong>&nbsp;The shift. Pigmentation lifts. Breakout frequency reduces. Barrier resilience improves. Makeup starts sitting better too, not because of the makeup, but because the skin underneath it is. This is the clean-girl look from the layer up.</p><p><strong>Day 60-75:</strong>&nbsp;Longevity. This is not the end. It's the new baseline. You're maintaining from a structurally different starting point now.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
    
  
    
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button type="button" class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-q-text">Why so slow? Other brands promise results in one or two weeks.</span>
            <span class="faq-icon"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <div class="faq-a-inner">
              <p>Nothing is wrong with us. A one-week result is almost always surface water and light-bouncing. It sits on top of the skin and leaves with your next wash. Real change happens at the cell-turnover and barrier level, and skin renews on its own clock, not a marketing one.</p><p>A two-week promise is a promise about the surface. Our 75 days is a promise about the structure. We'd rather under-promise the timeline and over-deliver the skin.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
    
  
    
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button type="button" class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-q-text">How do I use the serum?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <div class="faq-a-inner">
              <p>3-4 drops. AM and PM. Apply to clean, slightly damp skin. Let it absorb for 60 seconds before moisturiser. That's the protocol.</p><p>No skipping. The compound effect depends on consistency. A missed day doesn't break the protocol, but a missed week sets the clock back.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
    
  
    
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button type="button" class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-q-text">Can I use it with my existing routine?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <div class="faq-a-inner">
              <p>Yes. The LNGVTY serum goes after cleansing and before moisturiser. The bio-retinoids are sun-safe, so no changes to your SPF routine are needed.</p><p>If you're using prescription actives (tretinoin, clindamycin, etc.), consult your dermatologist before introducing any new serum.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
